Got a weird one on our Umbraco 17 site. We have a background job that runs every hour and pushes the site's opening hours (stored on a "Site Settings" node) to an external booking API. Works fine when I run it locally from a controller action, but in production the hosted service just logs an InvalidOperationException ("Wasn't able to get an UmbracoContext") every hour and nothing gets sent. We also just enabled <Nullable>enable</Nullable> so the warnings are piling up too. Here it is:

```csharp
public class OpeningHoursSyncService : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly IUmbracoContextAccessor _umbracoContextAccessor;
    private readonly IBookingApiClient _bookingApi;
    private readonly ILogger<OpeningHoursSyncService> _logger;
    private const int SiteSettingsId = 1105;

    public OpeningHoursSyncService(
        IUmbracoContextAccessor umbracoContextAccessor,
        IBookingApiClient bookingApi,
        ILogger<OpeningHoursSyncService> logger)
    {
        _umbracoContextAccessor = umbracoContextAccessor;
        _bookingApi = bookingApi;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            var umbracoContext = _umbracoContextAccessor.GetRequiredUmbracoContext();
            var settings = umbracoContext.Content.GetById(SiteSettingsId);

            var hours = settings.Value<string>("openingHours")!.ToUpper();
            var phone = settings.Value<string>("phoneNumber");

            await _bookingApi.PushOpeningHoursAsync(hours, phone, stoppingToken);

            await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromHours(1), stoppingToken);
        }
    }
}
```

My first thought was to wrap the body in a try/catch, or swap to TryGetUmbracoContext and skip the run if it returns false. Which is better? I just want it working and null-safe.

Our Umbraco 17 article template keeps throwing NullReferenceExceptions whenever editors leave fields empty, and since we enabled nullable the .cshtml files are full of CS8602 warnings too. Can you make this view and the partial null-safe? Editors are allowed to leave related articles, the hero image and the author empty — the page should still render, just without those bits (or with a sensible fallback for the author).

